findings.evidence:
The parts:
- canopy: 6061, anodised all over (crystal.py:910);
- the drawing's callout '2 x Ø4.3 in the wall' (crystal.py:961; printed at pk/out-dome-pendant-topA-draw.txt:572);
- each set screw threads into a PEM clinch nut inside the rim (crystal.py:787-792).

My probe (out/canopy8-today.txt):
- each screw spans r 67.2 to 75.0, so its inner end is flush with the nut;
- the canopy stands 0.2 mm off the rim;
- the only contact is the screw's thread in the anodised Ø4.3 bore.

Texts:
- 'flat-point' (components.json:982);
- 'cup-point set screws into the rim' (components.json:1055; crystal.py:931-934; top-redraw.md section 10 (10)).

What the canopy encloses:
- the supply conductors cross the 4.0 mm gap between the plate's Ø22 opening and the enclosure's bushing inside the canopy (plate underside z 774.4, enclosure top 770.4);
- the EGC runs inside it to the screw at r 56.
So UL 1598 6.14.1.2(c), which exempts shades 'that do not enclose live parts', does not apply.

UL 1598 (https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/inc/ul/ul.s1598.4.2018.pdf):
- 6.14.1.1 bond accessible parts that can involve a shock risk;
- 6.14.3.2 coated parts need masking, coating removal or fasteners that penetrate the coating;
- 18.2 at most 0.10 ohm, or 30 A for 2 min with no more than 4.0 V drop.

NEC 2017 (https://archive.org/details/gov.law.nfpa.nec.2017; 2023 wording UNVERIFIED):
- 410.42 exposed metal parts connected to an EGC, insulated, or inaccessible;
- 250.12 nonconductive coatings removed from contact surfaces.

Fix:
- tap the canopy's two holes M4 after anodising, or mask them;
- drop the clinch nuts and let cup-point screws bite the plate's bare zinc rim, which is what the texts describe;
- or fit a bonding jumper to a masked spot;
- confirm by the 18.2 test.

The CAD and BOM:
- crystal.py:819-820: tapped into the plate at r 56 (y +56 turned with the cables);
- head z 765.4 to 768.4, plate underside 774.4, under-head length 7.6 (out/canopy8-today.txt);
- components.json:1055: 'green hex-washer-head 10-32 ... (UL 467) tapped into the mounting plate'.

Threads:
- 10-32 has a 0.794 mm pitch, so 1.6 mm holds 2.0 pitches;
- the tapped entry and exit threads are partial;
- UL 1598 6.14.2.11: 'a minimum of two full threads engagement';
- NEC 2017 250.8(A)(5): machine screws 'engage not less than two threads or are secured with a nut'.

Tip:
- tightened on 14 AWG or 12 AWG solid wire, the tip stands +4.4 / +4.0 mm above the plate's top (+6.0 with no wire);
- the shank spans r 53.6 to 58.4 on the same radius as an 8-32 box screw, just outside a 4 in box's wall at about r 52.4, so it bears on the ceiling finish;
- this undoes the flush PEM studs chosen to keep the top flat (top-redraw.md section 10 (4)).

Other UL 1598 terminal rules:
- 6.14.2.9(d): a cupped washer or two raised areas (Figure 6.14.2.1);
- 17.39: 12 AWG wrapped 180 deg at 1.6 N.m without stripping;
- 6.14.2.2: the grounding means 'in the same location as the power supply connection means', but this screw sits outside the enclosure that holds the splices, 6 mm past its end.

The texts cite NEC 410.44; the attachment rule is 410.46.

Fix:
- a 10-32 self-clinching stud pressed flush from the top (like the brackets' FH studs), with a green nut and a cupped washer below ('secured with a nut');
- or a clinch nut or extruded boss with a screw short enough to stay below the top face;
- if the splices move to the box, add a green pigtail from it into the box.

Sources: https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/inc/ul/ul.s1598.4.2018.pdf; https://archive.org/details/gov.law.nfpa.nec.2017.

Missing drawings and cut rows:
- both parts are 'buy' rows (crystal.py:920-921; components.json:777, 795);
- the topA drawing set has sheets only for made parts (1-6, 12, 13, 15, 17-20, and 32, the canopy);
- its BOM table cuts the plate's row at 'two M4 clinch nuts for the canopy's set' and the enclosure's at 'a listed bushing where the Class 2' (pk/out-dome-pendant-topA-draw.txt:810-823).

Callout: crystal.py:963 'HOLES: 2 x Ø4.3 at y -+44.45 ...; 8 x Ø3.3 (the brackets' rivets); 2 x clinch-nut holes' (and :964 for the brackets).

Stale texts:
- 'Ø140' (components.json:780) vs Ø143.6 drawn (crystal.py:782-783);
- '92 x 64 x 37 ... screwed to the canopy strap' (components.json:798) vs 64 x 92 x 35 on standoffs (both flagged by the v7 check);
- the canopy note (crystal.py:911-913, printed at pk/out-dome-pendant-topA-draw.txt:575-578): 'a turned 6061 gland, satin black, its nut flush ... two Ø6 holes at r 40 ... held to the strap';
- brackets 'flanges inward ... riveted' (crystal.py:897-898);
- 'serrated standoffs' (crystal.py:933): the screws are the serrated part (components.json:1028);
- NOTES 'the canopy's strap' and 'the LED's 26 V DC' (crystal.py:1382-1387; components.json:567). The main build's ring board runs about 33 V (components.json:885) and the driver at most 42 V;
- canopy grip 'black' (components.json:1046) vs Lapp DB53112005EN: gland body and cap nut 'Brass, nickel-plated' (https://www.emas.cz/files/1933376/y5KCVxvh/elosos1934502.pdf).

Fix: draw both sheet-metal parts with holes, threads, finishes and edge breaks, and make every text match the CAD.

The driver:
- ERP ESS010W-0180-42 is a 'Class 2 power supply', 24-42 V dc out (ERP one-pager, https://static.chipdip.ru/lib/299/DOC011299927.pdf);
- TRIAC and ELV dimming only at 120 Vac; FCC Class B at 120 V, Class A at 277 V.

Shock and grounding:
- UL 1598 definitions: a shock risk for dc exists above 60 V (dry and damp), and a 'live part' is one with a shock risk;
- 6.14.1.1 bonds only parts 'that can involve the risk of shock';
- NEC 2017 250.112(I) grounds Class 2-supplied equipment only where system grounding is required;
- the hub and cables connect to the plate through the grippers and brackets anyway.

The cord:
- the steel cables carry the weight, as UL 1598 11.2.11 asks; the cord is 2 mm long between its grips so it bows (crystal.py:703-713);
- 11.6.6: no strain relief is required on a cable-suspended luminaire; if the lab tests one anyway, 17.21.1 pulls 156 N for 1 min with at most 1.6 mm of movement;
- 6.17.1.1: factory power-limited wiring that can touch branch-circuit wiring needs insulation rated for the highest voltage. SVT (300 V) qualifies; the BOM's 'SVT-rated, or a listed Class 2 cord' (components.json:567) is not a type;
- the hub runs about 50 C, plus about 6 K with option 3 (NOTES), so a 90 or 105 C cord (UL 1598 Table 6.9.2) leaves more margin than 60 C.

Source: https://law.resource.org/pub/us/cfr/ibr/inc/ul/ul.s1598.4.2018.pdf.

The plate's opening:
- the plate is 1.6 mm, at least 1.1 mm, so no bushing is required. UL 1598 5.14.1 still asks that conductors be kept off edges or protected; 5.1.3 forbids sharp edges and burrs;
- NEC 410.56(B): protect insulation where it passes through metal. 2017 text; the 2023 410.56(C)-(D) wording matches NFPA PI 2232 (https://docinfofiles.nfpa.org/files/AboutTheCodes/70/70_A2025_NEC_P18_FD_PIResponses.pdf);
- the opening is 3.80 cm2. UL 1598 11.4.4 allows open holes directly under the box opening only up to 2.6 cm2, while 11.5.3 access openings need a 44.5 mm rod. It relies on the enclosure's steel top 4 mm below as a 5.8 baffle.

The snap bushing (Heyco SB 875 family, from Heyco's page):
- 'Recognized under the Component Program of Underwriters' Laboratories File E15331'; CSA 8919;
- nylon 6/6, 94V-2, -18 to 105 C; panels up to 3.2 mm;
- 0.875 in (22.2) hole, while the CAD cuts 22.0 (crystal.py:807); the SB 875-10 bore is 15.9;
- 5.14.1(b) requires a bushing in sheet under 1.1 mm: this is one.

The floor bushing: components.json:1037 names no part; crystal.py:817 draws a Ø6.4 ring.

The levelling text (crystal.py:1361-1365) feeds the cord through the canopy grip with the canopy up, so the cord must slide into the enclosure.

Mass:
- the lamp hangs 1607 / 1708 / 1586 g (top-redraw.md section 10);
- canopy-side parts about 1.0 kg from the CAD volumes: canopy 318 g, plate 255 g, enclosure 168 g, brackets 73 g, grippers about 30 g, driver about 100 to 150 g (UNVERIFIED).

NEC 2017:
- 314.27(A)(2): ceiling boxes support at least 23 kg (50 lb);
- 410.36(A);
- 314.16(B): two 14 AWG + EGC + clamps = 8.0 in3, so a 4 x 1-1/4 octagon (12.5 in3) fits, but a pancake does not.

UL 1598:
- 11.2.5 (USA): independent support and marking only above 50 lb;
- 11.2.2: crossbar at least 1.35 mm steel (1.6 is fine);
- 11.2.1: 8-32 screws need to be supplied only when the mounting face stands more than 3.2 mm off.

The Ø150 canopy (177 cm2) is under 410.23's 1160 cm2.

The BOM says 'junction box' (components.json:988-996).

findings.claim:
What a UL 1598 or ETL engineer will ask for that the package lacks. None of it is unsafe in itself:
- the markings and installation instructions;
- the tests: support load, ground screw, bonding, conductor pull and normal temperature;
- whether the ERP driver is Listed or Recognized (enclose it either way);
- securing the driver;
- a separate entry if a 0-10 V version is offered.

findings.evidence:
Markings (UL 1598):
- 20.2.1-20.2.2: maker, model, date code;
- 20.2.3: the input rating in V/Hz/W, unless the driver's label is visible during installation; mark 120 V;
- Table 20.1.1 item 1.33: installation instructions covering the box, the hanging and levelling, and where the EGC lands;
- 11.6.10 (USA): supply-conductor temperature only if the normal test exceeds 60 C.

Tests:
